// The Japan edition, served the same way and from the same directory as the
// global one — see wrangler.jsonc, which this is a copy of but for the name.
//
// Two files rather than one with an environment, because what differs between
// the two deployments is not how they are served but what was built: each is
// `bun run build:<edition>` followed by a deploy of ./dist, and the two never
// happen in the same working copy at the same time.
//
// The dashboard will offer to "keep settings consistent" by writing this
// worker's name into wrangler.jsonc, and may offer it as a pull request.
// REFUSE IT. It has found the file named wrangler.jsonc beside this one and
// compared its name to the worker being looked at; what this deployment
// actually uses is this file, named on the deploy command as
// `wrangler deploy -c wrangler.jp.jsonc`. Taking the offer renames the worker
// that serves choai.dev.
{
  "name": "choai-jp",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-15",
  // Answers on jp.choai.dev. The name is attached where the deployment is
  // rather than declared here, for the reason written in wrangler.jsonc.
  "workers_dev": false,
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application"
  }
}
